oming releases, rel01.1 through rel01.3 cover cat, sponge, and du respectively, and these don't have source code yet. I need to check the PRDs to understand the specific requirements before starting implementation on cat. Looking at the code targets more carefully, cmd/version is the only generation target in the magefiles release, but I don't see any cmd/ implementations in the source code yet. The format, sys, and testutils packages are already complete, so the earliest actual code work would need to start with implementing cmd/version or moving to a later release where there's more substantial work to do.\n\nI'm realizing I need the actual PRD documents to write proper tasks with specific requirements, but the project context only includes source code references, not the full PRD content itself. I can see that prd001-testutils, prd002-sys, and prd003-format are referenced in the source code comments, but without the actual PRD text, I can't map requirements precisely to tasks. I'm realizing the project context doesn't actually include the PRD content itself—only the source code structure. Since I need to anchor tasks to specific requirement IDs but can't see the actual PRD, I'll reference the files that the stitch agent will read (like docs/specs/product-requirements/prd-cat.yaml) and let it extract the specific requirements to anchor the task properly. I'm realizing the constraint about PRD requirement IDs is strict, but I don't actually have access to the PRD content itself—only that it exists in required_reading. Let me focus on cmd/cat as the starting point since it's the earliest incomplete utility, and I can structure the task to have the stitch agent reference the PRD file directly rather than trying to guess at specific requirement IDs I can't verify.\n\nLooking at what's actually provided in the source code, I notice some packages have partial implementations—pkg/format has color and column utilities but missing tests, and pkg/sys has platform-specific fileinfo implementations without test coverage. I can see PRD references embedded in comments like prd003-format R1.1-R1.4, which gives me a pattern to work with for the tasks I can construct.\n\nSince I'm constrained to picking just one task, I should focus on whichever gap would have the most impact—likely the column layout tests since that's foundational to the formatting package. Following alphabetical tie-breaking on primary file path, column.go comes before size.go, so the task should be tests for pkg/format column layout covering R1.1-R1.4. But I need to verify whether the test files actually exist—the source_code listing shows color_test.go but not column_test.go or size_test.go, which suggests they're missing, though the absence from the listing doesn't necessarily mean they don't exist elsewhere.\n\nGiven that column.go is present in the source code and the test file isn't shown, I'll propose writing tests for the column functionality since it's part of the earliest release and has exactly four PRD sub-requirements that can be tested independently.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the test structure: I'll create column_test.go with unit tests covering the Columns function's row-major distribution, the PadRight and PadLeft padding functions, and the displayWidth rune counting logic, plus edge cases like empty inputs and terminal width boundaries. I'm following the table-driven test patterns and parallel execution conventions from the existing color_test.go file, using an external test package format_test.\n\nI should also check if there's a test suite specification in the docs that I need to reference for rel00.0, though I need to be careful about the code status for that release.\n\nLooking at the code_status for uc002-format, it shows testdir as empty and testfiles as 0, which suggests either no test suite spec exists yet or the test directory hasn't been configured. Since the format package is foundational shared infrastructure, these are unit tests rather than differential tests, so I should focus on that distinction.\n\nLet me step back and look at the full picture of what rel00.0 actually covers: uc001-magefiles is excluded from generation except for cmd/version, and uc002-format has existing code like color.go and column.\n\ngo but only partial tests with color_test.go. Meanwhile uc003-sys has code but no visible tests, and uc004-testutils already has both code and tests. The highest-impact single task would be filling in the missing test files.\n\nBetween the options for format, column_test.go makes the most sense as it's alphabetically first and aligns with the R1.1 through R1.4 requirements, which stays within the 4 PRD sub-requirement constraint.\n\nNow I'm verifying the line count estimate — looking at color_test.go as a reference point at around 227 lines for 6 functions, column_test.go should land in the 150-200 range given fewer functions but more complex test cases for the Columns function, which fits the 150-250 target.\n\nI'm choosing column_test.go over size_test.go because it exercises more intricate layout logic across varying terminal widths, and the design decision to follow existing test patterns from color_test.go is grounded in the codebase itself rather than invented.\n\nFor required reading, I need the PRD to understand what to test, the implementation to see what functions exist, and the existing test file to match conventions. I'm not including a test suite spec since this is a unit test for a shared library, not a differential test for a command-line utility.\n\nThe file naming follows the convention of mirroring the source file, so column.go becomes column_test.go. Now I'm putting together the final task definition with the PRD requirements, the implementation file, and the test conventions from the existing color_test.go file. I'm laying out the design approach: using an external test package to match the existing color_test.go pattern, organizing tests with table-driven subtests, and accounting 